  <h1>What to Do With a Lemon</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Document every repair visit</h2><p>Keep all repair orders. Each repair order must list the defect you complained about and the work performed. If the dealer writes a vague description, insist on specificity — &ldquo;customer states engine stalls at highway speed&rdquo; is better than &ldquo;customer concern.&rdquo; Your repair orders are your evidence.</p>
  <h2>2. Send written notice to the manufacturer</h2><p>Some states require written notice to the manufacturer before filing a lemon law claim. Send a certified letter to the manufacturer&rsquo;s customer relations address describing the defect, repair history, and demanding repurchase or replacement. This starts any required notice period and creates a record.</p>
  <h2>3. Contact a lemon law attorney</h2><p>Lemon law attorneys typically work on fee-shifting (not contingency) — if you win, the manufacturer pays their fees. Many offer free consultations. Contact one early: attorneys can advise on whether you meet the qualification threshold and how to document the remaining repair attempts strategically.</p>
  <h2>4. Don&rsquo;t give up the vehicle during the claim</h2><p>Keep the vehicle and continue documenting defects. Surrendering the vehicle before settlement or judgment can complicate the process.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-lemon-law-works">how lemon law works</a> and <a href="/guides/lemon-law-misconceptions">common misconceptions</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
